@prefix chg: <https://semblance.dev/ns/change#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix change: <https://semblance.dev/change/> .
@prefix per: <https://semblance.dev/ns/persistence#> .

# What changed for whoever has to move with it: AGENTS.md, "When a component changes".

change:remembered-state-within a chg:Change ;
    rdfs:label "per:retention is a ret:Within, measured from the data's age"@en ;
    chg:issue <https://github.com/inferal-oss/semblance/issues/530> ;
    chg:motivation """Remembered state is data a person owns, yet its retention was a one-off: an xsd:duration on a
per:Policy, measured from the policy's last write, all or nothing. A policy written every day never expired
anything, however old; one left alone for longer than the duration lost everything at once, silently, and
the expired blob stayed in storage, skipped but never removed. Every other bounded thing in Semblance says
what it keeps with the ret: vocabulary (docs/RETENTION.md), with an exit for what leaves, a horizon that
counts it and an announcement when a person-facing horizon is crossed.""" ;
    chg:substance """per:retention now names a ret:Within (its duration in tree:value), and is measured from the data's
age: each stored triple's own, when it was stored or last changed. A stored triple older than the duration is
not restored at load, and leaves by the policy's ret:onExpiry:

- ret:Offload, the default for remembered state: it is set aside in the same storage (the policy's key with
  "~aside"), not restored, and comes back with ret:recall on the policy (Show older on its horizon), never
  over a value the graph holds now. ret:storageAmount bounds what is set aside; past it the oldest are
  dropped and counted.
- ret:Drop: it is removed from storage, and counted. Only when the policy says so.

Either way the storage's live copy is rewritten without it (the new copy written before anything is
removed; what could not be set aside is restored after all), a per:Expire member records it in
per:StateLog, and the policy's horizon (<policy>/~horizon, a ret:Horizon in urn:semblance:retention) says
how much was restored, set aside and dropped. With ret:announce true, the first time in a session either
happens is announced (ret:Offloaded or ret:Dropped raised on the policy; per:AnnounceSetAside and
per:AnnounceExpired say so, in the viewer's language).

A duration alone (per:retention "P30D") keeps working: it is short for a ret:Within of it with ret:onExpiry
ret:Drop, as it dropped before. What changes for it is the age it is measured from: a triple unchanged for
longer than the duration expires even when the policy was written since, and a policy left alone keeps what
was changed recently. Stored state written before this change has no ages; each of its triples is taken to
be as old as its last write, which is what was measured before. A policy with a retention now stores its
base oldest first, with the ages beside it ("ages", run-length) and the time of each chunk ("at"); a policy
without one stores what it did. The head script that shows remembered choices before the first paint skips
the expired lines the same way.""" ;
    chg:forwardGuide """Nothing to move in the graph: a per:retention duration means what it did, a dropping
ret:Within, measured from each triple's age. To keep what expires instead of dropping it, write the
retention as a node:

    ex:draft a per:Policy ; per:retention [ a ret:Within ; tree:value "P30D" ; ret:onExpiry ret:Offload ; ret:announce true ] .

A host that attaches Persistence passes its event bus (persistence.attach(runtime, { bus })) for the
announcements; without one, the horizon and the state log still say what left.""" ;
    chg:backwardGuide """A per:retention duration goes back to being measured from the policy's last write, so a
policy written regularly stops expiring old triples. A per:retention that names a ret:Within node is not
read by the earlier version: write its tree:value as the duration (per:retention "P30D") to keep a
retention. What was set aside ("~aside" keys) is not read by the earlier version and stays in storage until
the policy is forgotten; recall it first to keep it. Stored ages are ignored by the earlier version.""" ;
    chg:affects per:Policy .
